**Two Sum** asks for two distinct indices in an array whose values add up to a target. The optimal approach uses a hash map to remember each number’s index while scanning once: for each `x`, check whether `target - x` was seen before; if so, return the pair.

```csharp
using System;
using System.Collections.Generic;

public class Solution
{
    public int[] TwoSum(int[] nums, int target)
    {
        var seen = new Dictionary<int, int>();

        for (int i = 0; i < nums.Length; i++)
        {
            int need = target - nums[i];
            if (seen.TryGetValue(need, out int j))
                return new[] { j, i };

            if (!seen.ContainsKey(nums[i]))
                seen[nums[i]] = i;
        }

        return Array.Empty<int>();
    }
}
```

**Time:** `O(n)`  
**Space:** `O(n)`

**Two Sum** asks you to find **two distinct numbers in an array whose sum equals a target** and return their indices. The optimal approach uses a **hash map** to remember numbers seen so far; for each number `x`, check whether `target - x` was seen earlier.

```csharp
using System;
using System.Collections.Generic;

public class Solution
{
    public int[] TwoSum(int[] nums, int target)
    {
        var seen = new Dictionary<int, int>();

        for (int i = 0; i < nums.Length; i++)
        {
            int need = target - nums[i];
            if (seen.TryGetValue(need, out int j))
                return new[] { j, i };

            if (!seen.ContainsKey(nums[i]))
                seen[nums[i]] = i;
        }

        throw new ArgumentException("No valid pair found.");
    }
}
```

**Time:** O(n)  
**Space:** O(n)

**Two Sum**: given an array `nums` and a target `target`, return the indices of two distinct numbers whose sum equals `target`. The optimal approach uses a hash map to store each number’s index as you scan once; for each `x`, check whether `target - x` has already been seen.

```csharp
using System;
using System.Collections.Generic;

public class Solution
{
    public int[] TwoSum(int[] nums, int target)
    {
        var seen = new Dictionary<int, int>();
        for (int i = 0; i < nums.Length; i++)
        {
            int need = target - nums[i];
            if (seen.TryGetValue(need, out int j))
                return new[] { j, i };

            if (!seen.ContainsKey(nums[i]))
                seen[nums[i]] = i;
        }
        return Array.Empty<int>();
    }
}
```

**Complexity:** `O(n)` time, `O(n)` space.
